window.docContents[7].resolve({"/#evaluating-messages":{"contents":"Asking Lean to evaluate a function application that is missing an argument will lead to an error message.\nIn particular, the exampleyields a quite long error message:\n\nThis message occurs because Lean functions that are applied to only some of their arguments return new functions that are waiting for the rest of the arguments.\nLean cannot display functions to users, and thus returns an error when asked to do so.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Getting to Know Lean\u0009Evaluating Expressions","header":"1.1.1. Messages You May Meet","id":"/#evaluating-messages"},"/#prod":{"contents":"The  structure, short for “Product”, is a generic way of joining two values together.\nFor instance, a  contains a  and a .\nIn other words,  could be replaced by .\n is very much like C#'s tuples, the Pair and Triple types in Kotlin, and tuple in C++.\nMany applications are best served by defining their own structures, even for simple cases like , because using domain terminology can make it easier to read the code.\nAdditionally, defining structure types helps catch more errors by assigning different types to different domain concepts, preventing them from being mixed up.\n\nOn the other hand, there are some cases where it is not worth the overhead of defining a new type.\nAdditionally, some libraries are sufficiently generic that there is no more specific concept than “pair”.\nFinally, the standard library contains a variety of convenience functions that make it easier to work with the built-in pair type.\n\nThe structure  is defined with two type arguments:\n\nLists are used so frequently that there is special syntax to make them more readable.\nFor the same reason, both the product type and its constructor have special syntax.\nThe type  is typically written , mirroring the usual notation for a Cartesian product of sets.\nSimilarly, the usual mathematical notation for pairs is available for .\nIn other words, instead of writing:it suffices to write:\n\nBoth notations are right-associative.\nThis means that the following definitions are equivalent:In other words, all products of more than two types, and their corresponding constructors, are actually nested products and nested pairs behind the scenes.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Getting to Know Lean\u0009Polymorphism\u0009More Built-In Datatypes","header":"1.6.3.2. Prod","id":"/#prod"}});